  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nRidging and high pressure will keep the area dry and ~15F warmer\nthan average Saturday, though high clouds may encroach\nthroughout the day as moisture aloft advects in from a southeast\ncoastal low. This will progress north, along the eastern\nseaboard Sunday, dry and warm weather continues to be favored\nfor most. There is a low chance that some rain creeps into the \nhigher terrain of the Laurels and WV (20% to 50% chance), should\na spoke of PVA riding the Appalachians enhance some ascent. But\neven should this occur, reasonable high-end totals are less \nthan 0.25\" of QPF. \n\nA cold front is expected to pass Monday morning, which is \nexpected to bring high temperatures to more seasonable levels \nand provide more widespread rain chances, though again, even \nhigh-end QPF is less than 0.25\". Monday will not be a washout as\nshowers will likely just be tied to the frontal passage itself.\nSome lake-enhanced light precipitation may fall north of I-80 \nMonday night. Behind the cold front, highs Tuesday/Wednesday are\nfavored to be ~5 to 10 degrees below average.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThere has been some increased confidence in the overall \npattern, but there still resides a fairly large range of \npotential outcomes for a weather system favored to impact the \narea Thanksgiving into Black Friday. \n\nIn the last 24 hours of so, a smaller fraction of NBM ensemble\nmembership shows a notable snow accumulation event for this\nperiod. There is overall, a 75% to 95% chance of no snow\naccumulation for the lowlands and a less than 10% chance of \ngreater than an inch. Compare this to 25% to 40% of a non-zero \naccumulation and ~20% of greater than 1\" from 24 hours ago. \nChances also dropped of greater than 3\" for the ridges of PA/WV \ndown to 5-10% from 20-30% from 24 hours ago. What might describe\nthis change?\n\nEnsembles are a bit more confident in a scenario where a trough\ndrops off into the central CONUS, with an upper low closing off\nfrom the flow, slowing down as it does so. Ensembles have\ntrended toward a solution where this upper low maintains more \nof a northern track in the upper midwest, so when a coastal low \nrides up into the mid-Atlantic, low flow is still southerly with\nthe upper low anchoring nearer the Great Lakes. This may keep \nthe area too warm for precipitation to fall as snow, but may \nstill see a few flakes should the coastal low occlude and the \nregion flips to northwest flow Friday.\n\nThere are still fairly long tails in potential for snowfall\naccumulations, and there are still outlying ensemble members \nthat do show a more southern track upper low that occludes\nushering in more NW flow. Some of the snowiest outcomes rely on\na deep, cold trough with an upper low that does not close off, \na solution which is still technically possible, but is falling\nout of ensemble favor.",
